1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,934
A coronary angiogram shows where a heart
artery has narrowed.

2
00:00:05,538 --> 00:00:09,950
Coronary angioplasty can widen that
narrowed passage.

3
00:00:10,461 --> 00:00:15,592
In this episode, we’ll follow one example
from the first X-ray images to stent

4
00:00:15,673 --> 00:00:18,693
placement, the final check and recovery.

5
00:00:19,296 --> 00:00:23,545
Coronary arteries supply the heart muscle
with oxygen-rich blood.

6
00:00:24,149 --> 00:00:29,083
Plaque can build up in the artery wall and
narrow the space where blood flows.

7
00:00:29,687 --> 00:00:35,028
In some heart attacks, a clot forms at a
plaque and suddenly reduces or blocks

8
00:00:35,098 --> 00:00:36,038
blood flow.

9
00:00:36,735 --> 00:00:41,367
During an angiogram, contrast outlines the
artery on X-ray.

10
00:00:41,971 --> 00:00:46,824
The images show where the channel narrows
and help the team decide what comes next.

11
00:00:47,520 --> 00:00:52,420
If treatment is appropriate, angioplasty
can follow in the same session or be

12
00:00:52,478 --> 00:00:57,679
planned separately. The options may
include medicines, angioplasty or

13
00:00:57,760 --> 00:00:59,049
bypass surgery.

14
00:00:59,560 --> 00:01:04,819
Angioplasty is part of a treatment called
percutaneous coronary intervention, or P

15
00:01:04,993 --> 00:01:10,124
C I. The team can reach the heart through
a small sheath in an artery at the

16
00:01:10,194 --> 00:01:15,546
wrist or groin. In this example, a guide
catheter reaches the opening

17
00:01:15,604 --> 00:01:17,299
of the coronary artery.

18
00:01:17,903 --> 00:01:22,570
Now a fine coronary guidewire passes
through the guide catheter.

19
00:01:23,383 --> 00:01:28,758
The cardiologist watches on live X-ray as
the wire crosses the narrowing.

20
00:01:29,362 --> 00:01:34,320
Once the tip is beyond it, the wire acts
as a track for the balloon and stent

21
00:01:34,436 --> 00:01:35,922
delivery catheters.

22
00:01:36,734 --> 00:01:41,866
In this example, the team first prepares
the narrowing with a separate balloon.

23
00:01:42,563 --> 00:01:47,462
The balloon arrives folded, slides over
the guidewire and opens at the

24
00:01:47,543 --> 00:01:52,466
narrowed spot. As it inflates, it
stretches the artery and

25
00:01:52,547 --> 00:01:54,741
presses the plaque toward the wall.

26
00:01:55,345 --> 00:02:00,604
The team deflates and removes that first
balloon while the guidewire stays in

27
00:02:00,674 --> 00:02:05,341
place. This preparation step depends on
the narrowing.

28
00:02:05,782 --> 00:02:10,775
In suitable cases, the team can advance
the stent delivery system directly over

29
00:02:10,821 --> 00:02:11,645
the wire.

30
00:02:12,156 --> 00:02:13,677
Here comes the stent.

31
00:02:14,118 --> 00:02:18,913
It’s a tiny metal mesh tube, already
mounted around a second, folded

32
00:02:18,971 --> 00:02:24,242
balloon. The balloon and stent move
together over the guidewire.

33
00:02:24,938 --> 00:02:29,176
The cardiologist positions the assembly
across the narrowed section before

34
00:02:29,223 --> 00:02:30,267
expanding it.

35
00:02:30,964 --> 00:02:35,480
When the delivery balloon inflates, it
opens the mesh outward against the artery

36
00:02:35,538 --> 00:02:39,950
wall. The expanded stent supports the
wider passage.

37
00:02:40,194 --> 00:02:45,012
The team then deflates the balloon and
slides the balloon catheter back out.

38
00:02:45,964 --> 00:02:48,391
Watch the mesh stay in the artery.

39
00:02:49,203 --> 00:02:54,208
Many coronary stents have a coating that
gradually releases medicine into the

40
00:02:54,277 --> 00:02:55,833
area around the stent.

41
00:02:56,530 --> 00:02:59,037
These are called drug-eluting stents.

42
00:02:59,548 --> 00:03:03,879
The medicine helps lower the chance that
tissue growth will narrow the treated area

43
00:03:03,960 --> 00:03:04,633
again.

44
00:03:05,330 --> 00:03:10,473
The team takes another set of X-ray images
with contrast to check the treated segment

45
00:03:10,601 --> 00:03:13,619
and see how blood flows farther down the
artery.

46
00:03:14,130 --> 00:03:16,765
In this example, the channel is wider.

47
00:03:17,149 --> 00:03:21,700
The team can also use imaging from inside
the artery to examine how the stent has

48
00:03:21,769 --> 00:03:22,408
opened.

49
00:03:23,011 --> 00:03:27,226
After the team finishes its checks, it
removes the wire and catheter.

50
00:03:27,667 --> 00:03:31,881
When the sheath is removed, a pressure
band can help stop bleeding at the wrist.

51
00:03:32,485 --> 00:03:36,630
For a groin site, the team may use
pressure or a closure device.

52
00:03:37,141 --> 00:03:41,715
In recovery, the team checks your blood
pressure, pulse and access site.

53
00:03:42,156 --> 00:03:46,440
Complications can include bleeding, artery
injury or a clot.

54
00:03:46,951 --> 00:03:52,292
Your discharge plan explains wound care,
activity, medicines and follow-up.

55
00:03:52,989 --> 00:03:57,319
The timing depends on how you are doing
and whether the procedure was planned or

56
00:03:57,400 --> 00:03:58,724
followed a heart attack.

57
00:03:59,421 --> 00:04:04,134
After a stent, your care team usually
prescribes antiplatelet medicines.

58
00:04:04,645 --> 00:04:09,254
These make platelets less likely to clump
together and help prevent a clot inside

59
00:04:09,289 --> 00:04:14,606
the stent. The choice and duration depend
on your situation, including your risk of

60
00:04:14,665 --> 00:04:19,947
bleeding. Take them as prescribed and
contact your cardiology team before

61
00:04:20,005 --> 00:04:21,747
stopping or changing them.

62
00:04:22,443 --> 00:04:26,344
Follow the activity and wound-care
instructions from your hospital.

63
00:04:27,041 --> 00:04:31,569
Get emergency help for severe or
persistent chest pain, bleeding that won’t

64
00:04:31,627 --> 00:04:36,503
stop, or a hand or foot that becomes cold
or numb on the side used for the

65
00:04:36,538 --> 00:04:41,739
procedure. Ask your team about follow-up
and cardiac rehabilitation.

66
00:04:42,250 --> 00:04:45,245
The stent supports one treated section of
the artery.

67
00:04:45,524 --> 00:04:48,786
Coronary artery disease still needs
ongoing care.

68
00:04:49,297 --> 00:04:54,162
Ask your care team what was treated, which
medicines to take and when your recovery

69
00:04:54,231 --> 00:04:57,587
plan allows you to return to usual
activities.
